# The word classes named with their proper terms and each defined by its job in a sentence: noun,
# verb, adjective, adverb, pronoun, preposition and conjunction, with the subject of a verb, and the
# same word changing class when its job changes. Every sentence is set at the observatory.
lesson writing-naming-the-words v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=5 subject=writing levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Naming the words"
  goal "Name the word class of any word in a sentence by the job it does there."

  look {
    scene 34x16 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        wordcards cards words=["The", "comet", "moved", "slowly"] picked=-1
        row pics space=2 align=end {
          observatory dome open=1
          comet c tail=6
        }
      }
    }
    say """
      Every word in a sentence does a job, and each job has a name. A noun names a person, place or
      thing: comet. A verb says what happens: moved. An adjective tells you about a noun, and an
      adverb tells you how, when or where something happens: slowly. A pronoun stands in for a noun,
      a preposition places one thing by another, and a conjunction joins two parts. A word can
      change its job, so read the whole sentence first.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked writing.name-the-class k=0 level=medium
    }
    show writing.name-the-class k=6 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    show writing.name-the-class k=2 easy-k=6 hard-k=5
    show writing.gap-class k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show writing.adjective-or-adverb k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
  }
  story {
    show writing.same-word-new-job k=0 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show writing.subject-and-verb k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show writing.same-word-new-job k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=7
    show writing.subject-and-verb k=4 easy-k=2
    level hard {
      show writing.gap-class k=3
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show writing.which-class-reason k=0 easy-k=2 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show writing.pattern-sentence k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "A word's class is the job it does in its sentence: read the whole sentence, then name the job."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The lesson names the word classes with the terms schools use, where earlier grades said naming
    word, doing word and describing word: noun, verb, adjective, adverb, pronoun, preposition and
    conjunction, with the subject as the noun that does the verb. Each is defined by its job in a
    sentence, not by a list to learn, because the same word changes class with its job: fast is an
    adjective in a fast comet and an adverb in moved fast, and after is a preposition before a noun
    (after the eclipse) and a conjunction before a clause with its own verb (after the moon had
    set). The way in names a picked word, and the core names a gap's class from its place, sorts
    adjectives from adverbs, decides a word's class from its sentence and finds the verb and its
    subject. The wrong idea to watch for is naming a word by a habit: every -ly word an adverb,
    every describing word an adjective, every word for a thing a noun. Ask "what does it go with
    here?" If the child is stuck, cover the word with a finger and ask what the sentence has lost.
    Off the page, take a sentence from a book at bedtime and name the class of every word in it. The
    two-star question corrects a friend who names a class by habit, and the three-star is sentences
    built to a pattern, which you mark. Russia's federal work programme for grade five teaches the
    parts of speech by name in its «Морфология» section, and the Common Core (L.5.1a) asks fifth
    graders to explain the function of "conjunctions, prepositions, and interjections".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level naming a verb is worked once first, the words to name are a noun, an adjective and a pronoun, the gaps and the sort have plain adjectives and -ly adverbs, the same word changes between noun and verb or adjective and adverb, the subject comes before its verb, and the two-star is about light."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are an adverb and a conjunction, the sort puts friendly and lovely among the adjectives, after and before change between preposition and conjunction, one subject comes after its verb and one hides behind another noun, a gap needs a preposition, and the two-star is about after."
  }
}
